---
title: ':shallow_pan_of_food: A Centerpiece Squash'
source: https://cupofjo.com/2026/11/11/the-25-dinner-thanksgiving-edition
author: Casey Elsass
servings: 8 to 10
cook time: 55 minutes
total time: 1 hour 10 minutes
tags:
  - butternut-squash
  - cup-of-jo
  - main
  - thanksgiving
---

Preheat the oven to 425°F.

Use a #vegetable peeler{} to peel @butternut squash{1%large} (about 4 lbs), continuing past the pale outer layer and any green stripes until you reach the bright orange flesh. Cut the squash in half lengthwise, then use a #soup spoon{} to scoop out the seeds and stringy flesh.

On a #rimmed baking sheet{}, rub the squash all over with @extra-virgin olive oil{2%Tbsp} and season both sides generously with @kosher salt{} and @freshly ground black pepper{}. Turn the squash halves cut-side down and pour @water{1/4%cup} onto the baking sheet. Roast until the squash is slightly soft but still holds its shape, about ~{20%minutes}. Remove from the oven and cool for about ~{15%minutes}, or until the baking sheet is comfortable to handle.

Place a pair of #chopsticks{} (or two wooden spoon handles) along both sides of one squash half. Using a #sharp knife{}, cut crosswise into 1/4-inch-thick slices, letting the chopsticks stop you from cutting all the way through. Repeat with the second squash half. Brush the top of the squash with @extra-virgin olive oil{1%Tbsp} and return to the oven. Roast until the squash is completely tender and the tops are lightly caramelized, 30 to ~{35%minutes}.

Meanwhile, in a #small saucepan{}, heat the remaining @extra-virgin olive oil{1%Tbsp} over medium heat. Stir in @shallot{1%small} (finely diced) and cook until softening, about ~{2%minutes}. Add @packed light brown sugar{3%Tbsp}, @Dijon mustard{2%Tbsp}, @apple cider vinegar{2%Tbsp}, and @water{2%Tbsp}. Using a #whisk{}, whisk well to combine, then let bubble while whisking occasionally until the bubbles turn thick and slow, 3 to ~{4%minutes}. Remove from the heat and whisk again until glossy and syrupy (return to heat for 1 to ~{2%minutes} if needed). Taste and season with salt and pepper.

Slide a #fish spatula{} (or a long offset spatula or long chef's knife) under the squash to release it cleanly from the baking sheet. Transfer the squash halves to a #serving platter{}, then spoon the warm glaze over the top so it slides down the sides. Sprinkle with @walnuts{1/4%cup} (toasted and finely chopped), @pomegranate seeds{1/4%cup}, and @fresh chives{} (thinly sliced) just before serving.
